Verification result for the flexGrow/flexShrink base style (branch fix/scrollable-height)

Static gates pass. Full suite: 1599 passed, 1 expected fail, **2 FAILED** —
both in tests/gtk/components/viewability.gtk.test.tsx:

  expected [0..9] to deeply equal [0..4]

The test renders <FlatList style={{ height: 200 }}> inside a 400x400 Root and
expects a 200px viewport (rows 0-4). With the base style applied it reports a
400px viewport (rows 0-9).

Cause: `flexGrow: 1` and an explicit `height` do not conflict as properties,
so composing the style later does NOT protect the height. In flexbox an
explicit main-axis size is the flex BASIS, and grow expands from there into
free space — 200 basis in a 400 container grows to 400. So the base style
silently overrides every explicit ScrollView height in a bounded flex parent.

What is NOT yet established, and must be before this can ship: whether React
Native behaves the same way. RN composes the same base style under the app's,
so `<ScrollView style={{height: 200}}>` in a bounded column may well grow to
400 there too — in which case our test encodes behaviour RN does not have and
the TEST is what should change. Decide it by checking RN, not by reasoning:
this session was wrong three times reasoning about what upstream does.

Next step: run the same shape on real RN (or read ScrollView.js + its Yoga
config) and then either
  (a) narrow the base style so an explicit main-axis size wins, or
  (b) change the two viewability tests and document the RN-parity behaviour.
